data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_0a49ae7ae08958c5006a51bb16dd0c819594008b1b221eb8bb","logprobs":[],"output_index":2,"sequence_number":449,"text":"Microsoft Foundry Agent Service is Microsoft’s managed platform for building, deploying, and scaling AI “agents” (AI applications that can reason over requests and take multi-step actions using tools and data—not just generate text). It’s designed so you can use models from the Foundry model catalog and connect agents to built-in and custom tools, with a single API entry point via the Responses API.  \n\n### What it provides (key building blocks)\n- **Responses API (single entry point):** Use it from “any framework, process, or runtime” to access Foundry models and platform tools (for example: file search, code interpreter, memory, web search, MCP servers).  \n- **Agent Runtime:** A managed runtime that hosts and scales agents, manages conversations/tool calls, and handles lifecycle concerns.  \n- **Tools:** Built-in tools (web/file search, memory, code interpreter, MCP servers, custom functions) plus managed authentication patterns (including Entra and OBO scenarios).  \n- **Observability & security:** Tracing/metrics integrations (including Application Insights) and enterprise controls like RBAC, content filtering, and network isolation.  \n\n### Agent types you can build\n- **Prompt agents:** Define instructions/prompts in the Foundry portal or via SDK/REST; Foundry runs it without you managing app code/containers/compute.  \n- **Hosted agents:** Bring agent code (using frameworks like LangGraph or SDKs) packaged as a container; Foundry provides the managed endpoint, scaling, identity, and observability.  \n\n### Note on “classic” vs the current service\nMicrosoft’s documentation distinguishes older **Foundry Agent Service (classic)** agents (deprecated and scheduled to retire **March 31, 2027**) versus the newer generally available agents experience. The classic docs also describe a broad tool ecosystem (knowledge tools like Azure AI Search; action tools like Logic Apps, OpenAPI, MCP, function calling, etc.).  \n\nIf you tell me your scenario (internal copilot, customer support, automation/back-office workflows, etc.), I can recommend which agent type (prompt vs hosted) and which tools to start with."}

